## Narrow view - scroll containers measured on the element itself, per the G3 reference

The reference's warning was followed exactly: each <table> and each <figure> was measured
directly, not the wrapping div.theme-doc-markdown, and each was then driven to
el.scrollLeft = el.scrollWidth and re-measured.

On every one of the 8 pages the wrapper div.theme-doc-markdown reports
scrollWidth 328 == clientWidth 328 and computed overflow-x: visible, which is the correct,
non-defective reading the reference describes. documentElement and body horizontal overflow
were 0 on all 8 pages in all 3 setups.

Figures at 360px (10 of 10, two per topic file):
  fig-U3-1  scrollWidth 900 / clientWidth 328   fig-U3-2  900/328
  fig-U3-3  900/328                             fig-U3-4  800/328
  fig-U3-5  880/328                             fig-U3-6  880/328
  fig-U3-7  860/328                             fig-U3-8  860/328
  fig-U3-9  900/328                             fig-U3-10 900/328
  all: computed overflow-x auto; scrollLeft accepted; after scrollLeft = scrollWidth the
  right edge was reached (scrollLeft + clientWidth == scrollWidth); tabindex="0",
  role="region" and a non-empty aria-label all present from DocItem/Content hydration.
  => reachable by swipe AND from a keyboard. Not a defect.

Tables at 360px:
  topic-01..topic-05 mini-rubrics: scrollWidth 328 == clientWidth 328, no overflow, and the
  hydration correctly REMOVED tabindex/role/aria-label (they are not scroll regions).
  unit-assessment ERQ rubrics, 5 tables: scrollWidth 527, 473, 471, 488, 499 inside
  clientWidth 328; all scrolled to their right edge; all carry tabindex="0", role="region"
  and an aria-label. Not a defect.

## Print view (A4)

No horizontal overflow on any page. Every figure and table renders at 823px inside an 823px
box, i.e. nothing is cut. Per topic, 4 <img> nodes exist and exactly 2 are visible: that is
the intended Figure light/dark pair, one hidden per [data-theme]. Both carry the identical
alt, so exactly one is exposed to the accessibility tree. The unit-assessment print view
carries the full question bank AND the whole "Answers and marking guidance" section,
including all five ERQ rubric tables, with nothing clipped.

## Structural accessibility

Exactly one <h1> per page. No heading-level skips on any of the 8 pages in any setup.
20 <img> nodes across the unit, every one with a non-empty alt; the shortest is 150
characters and each matches its row in specs/content/efmp-302/figures/unit-03.md verbatim.
No vague link text ("here", "click here", "read more", ...) on any page.

## Glyph geometry

node scripts/measure-figure-text.mjs over all 20 committed Unit 3 SVGs: exit 0, no viewBox
overflow and no text-on-wordmark overprint (measure-figure-text.log).

That tool compares <text> against <text> only. A separate pass measuring EVERY painted
element against the .wm wordmark box (wordmark-occlusion.log) found one real collision that
both check:figures and measure-figure-text.mjs pass silently:
  fig-U3-6.svg and fig-U3-6.dark.svg - rect.o2 (the "Escalated to the head" outcome box,
  box [640,440,850,504]) intersects the wordmark box [775.7,493.6,868.6,506.8] by
  74.3 x 10.4 px. The wordmark is later in document order, so it paints ON TOP of the box
  and the box's bottom stroke runs through the glyphs.
  Captured at 3x in renders-.../fig-U3-6-wordmark-collision-3x.png.
  The instructional labels are unaffected and fully legible, and the wordmark is
  aria-hidden="true" branding, so a learner loses no instructional meaning. Advisory.

## Figure content inspected as content, not only as pixels

All 10 schematics were rendered standalone at 2x (fig-U3-N-render-2x.png) and their text
nodes extracted from the committed SVG bytes and compared against the prose of the topic
that carries them. Two contradictions between a figure and its own topic were found and are
recorded as blocking findings in the report; see B-01 and B-02 on fig-U3-7.
